    "lens": {
      "mechanics": "When credit and money fund spending that grows faster than output, prices can rise. Gold pays no interest, so higher returns on bonds after inflation raise the cost of holding bullion.",
      "data": "Three observations frame the holiday. <a href=\"https://bea.gov/sites/default/files/2026-11/pi1026.pdf\">October core PCE inflation was 3.3% year on year</a>, keeping pressure on policymakers to contain prices. <a href=\"https://home.treasury.gov/resource-center/data-chart-center/interest-rates/TextView?type=daily_treasury_real_yield_curve&amp;field_tdr_date_value_month=202611\">The latest 10-year real yield was 2.73% on 25 Nov</a>. That decline had reduced gold’s opportunity cost before the holiday. <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/T10YIE\">Ten-year market inflation compensation held at 2.12% on 25 Nov</a>. The last cash readings predate Thursday’s modest gold decline. Kitco reported a firmer dollar and continued rate-hike expectations during the holiday session.",
